<article class="tm-article">
  <nav class="tm-breadcrumb"><a href="/hc/en-us">Help Center</a> / Frequently asked questions</nav>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>Is biogas really CO₂-neutral?</h3>
    <p>Yes. The CO₂ released when biogas is used comes from plants and animals and is part of the natural cycle – not from fossil sources. At the same time, methane is captured from waste that would otherwise have escaped into the atmosphere.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>What is biogas made from?</h3>
    <p>From organic residual products: livestock manure, straw, food waste and residual products from the food industry. None of it is grown specifically for energy – it is material that would otherwise be discarded.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>Do I need to change anything in my home to use green gas?</h3>
    <p>No. Biomethane has the same quality as natural gas and is fed into the same gas grid. Your gas boiler and stove work exactly as before. Read more under <a href="/hc/en-us/p/groen-gas-til-hjemmet">Green gas for the home</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>Does a biogas plant smell?</h3>
    <p>Modern plants are closed systems precisely in order to minimise odour. In fact, biogas reduces odour nuisance in agriculture, because the digested manure smells less than untreated manure when it is spread on the fields.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>What happens to the residue?</h3>
    <p>The digested biomass is a nutrient-rich fertiliser product that is returned to farmers and replaces synthetic fertiliser. This turns production into a closed, circular cycle.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>What does Shell have to do with Danish biogas?</h3>
    <p>In 2023 Shell acquired the Danish company Nature Energy – Europe's largest producer of biomethane. Read more under <a href="/hc/en-us/p/tm-og-nature-energy">Shell &amp; Nature Energy</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tm-faq-item">
    <h3>Can biogas be used for transport?</h3>
    <p>Yes. As compressed gas (Bio-CNG) or liquefied gas (BioLNG), biogas can power buses, trucks and ships. See the article <a href="/hc/en-us/p/biogas-til-transport">Biogas &amp; BioLNG for transport</a>.</p>
  </div>

